        "testata": "The Hill",
        "lingua_originale": "en",
        "titolo_orig": "Live updates: Trump backs Paxton for Texas Senate; Blanche says 'anybody' can apply to compensation fund",
        "testo_it": "Aggiornamenti politici USA: Trump sostiene Paxton per il Senato in Texas.",
        "priorita": "Media",
        "id_gruppo": 22,
        "link": "https://thehill.com/homenews/administration/5883563-trump-blanche-kentucky-primary-thomas-massie/",
        "orario": "18:41",
        "timestamp": 1779208860,
        "contenuto_esteso": "LIVE UPDATES Administration Live updates: Trump backs Paxton for Texas Senate; Vance leads White House press briefing by The Hill Staff - 05/19/26 12:54 PM ET by The Hill Staff - 05/19/26 12:54 PM ET President Trump on Tuesday backed Attorney General Ken Paxton in the Texas Senate GOP primary over incumbent John Cornyn. “Ken is a true MAGA Warrior who has ALWAYS delivered for Texas, and will continue to do so in the United States Senate,” Trump wrote on Truth Social. Paxton and Cornyn will meet in a runoff next Tuesday, with the winner facing off against state Rep. James Talarico (D). At the White House, Vice President Vance will brief reporters at 1 p.m. in place of press secretary Karoline Leavitt. On the Hill, acting Attorney General Todd Blanche led a long line of Cabinet secretaries and administration officials testifying on Capitol Hill on Tuesday. Asked about the $1.776 billion fund announced Monday to compensate those who claim to have been caught in investigations under the Biden administration, Blanche said anyone in the U.S. could apply to the fund and a commission would determine payouts. “That’s not for me to set, that’s for the commissioners, and whether an individual, an Oath Keeper, as you just mentioned, applies for compensation — anybody in this country can apply,” he told Sen. Chris Coons (D-Del.). The battle between Trump and Rep. Thomas Massie (R-Ky.) will reach a fever pitch Tuesday, as Massie tries to buck a recent trend and hang on to his seat in what has become the most expensive House primary — ever. The president told reporters on Tuesday that he doesn’t think Massie is a Republican. Voters in a handful of other states, including Alabama, Georgia and Pennsylvania, will also head to the polls Tuesday. Here’s what to watch . Follow live below. Add as preferred source on Google Copyright 2026 Nexstar Media Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. More Administration News See All See All Video/Hill.TV See all Hill.TV See all Video See all Hill.TV See all Video Top Stories See All See All"
